# Regulatory & Political Analyst — Brief

**Run:** quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2
**Author lens:** Twenty years at the intersection of airport management, FAA/TSA/CBP, and the Hill. Politics and regulation are usually more binding than the economics — and at MWAA more than anywhere else, because MWAA is federally chartered and Congress has never let go.

---

## 1. Key findings (lead with the operative fact)

1. **NFPA 72 (2022) fixes the floor: STI ≥ 0.45 or CIS ≥ 0.7 in every area where voice evacuation is required.** A "quiet-by-design" standard that reduces speakers, coverage, or SPL without a compliance proof for the emergency-communication envelope will fail plan review — and it will fail it in Fairfax County (IAD) and Arlington County (DCA) fire marshal offices, not at MWAA. This is regulation, not preference. [Source: NFPA 72 §18.4.11 / Annex D]

2. **ADA Title II, 28 CFR §35.160, requires "as effective" communication and gives "primary consideration" to the individual's requested aid.** A terminal that shifts information onto smartphones as the default channel is legally exposed unless equal-quality visual, tactile, and staffed channels are specified as part of the standard, not layered on later. [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-28/chapter-I/part-35/subpart-E/section-35.160]

3. **DCA's slot and perimeter regime is statutory, not administrative — and it just moved for the fifth time since 1966.** Section 502 of the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 (P.L. 118-63) authorized 10 additional beyond-perimeter slot exemptions at DCA. That is the tempo of change: a decade of lobbying to move ten slots in a market that runs ~800 daily operations. Treat slot/perimeter as fixed inputs to any quiet-by-design workload analysis. [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12504]

4. **The PFC cap has been $4.50 since April 1, 2001 (P.L. 106-181, the Wendell H. Ford AIR-21 Act).** Every serious attempt to raise it in the last four reauthorization debates has died in the airline lobby. Do not build a quiet-by-design funding thesis on a PFC increase; build it on AIP/BIL Terminal Program eligibility, airline rate base under the 2025 Use & Lease, and PFC scope amendments within the existing $4.50 cap. [Source: https://www.faa.gov/airports/pfc]

5. **MWAA's Board is a four-jurisdiction political assembly, and the Supreme Court has already stripped it of one federal control mechanism.** *MWAA v. Citizens for the Abatement of Aircraft Noise* (501 U.S. 252, 1991) struck the original Board of Review as an unconstitutional violation of separation of powers. That history means every governance instrument at MWAA — including a design-manual revision that would bind two federally leased airports — is drafted with an eye to what a court, a Cruz-led committee, or a Warner-Kaine coalition can do to it. [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/501/252]

6. **FAA AC 150/5360-13A is guidance, not a rule — but it becomes de facto rule when MWAA takes AIP or BIL Terminal Program money.** Grant assurance conformance requires that design meets applicable ACs. A quiet-by-design standard is a supplement to the AC universe, not a substitute; it must be internally consistent with FAA terminal planning, TSA checkpoint standards, and CBP FIS technical design. [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC-150-5360-13A-Airport-Terminal-Planning.pdf]

7. **The next structural federal window is the FY2029 FAA Reauthorization.** The current authorization (P.L. 118-63) runs through FY2028. If a quiet-by-design standard needs any statutory change — PFC scope expansion, categorical funding for accessible communication infrastructure, or NEPA streamlining for interior-only work — the drafting window opens roughly Q1 2027 with committee markup. Anything on a shorter clock has to run on existing authority. [Source: https://www.faa.gov/about/reauthorization]

8. **The July 2026 Dulles announcement made IAD passenger-facing design federally visible property.** A joint White House / USDOT / MWAA / United announcement of a $20–22.5 billion transformation elevates political attention on any high-visibility interior standard. That helps a "safety-and-accessibility" quiet-by-design frame; it hurts a "premium calm for elite travelers" frame. Frame the standard as ADA/NFPA compliance plus operational resilience, not passenger amenity, or expect it to be characterized that way anyway.

