## 2. What binds, in the order it binds

### 2.1 Federal fire and life-safety code (non-negotiable)

**NFPA 72, National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code (2022 edition)**, is adopted by reference through the Virginia Statewide Fire Prevention Code and the D.C. Construction Codes. Section 18.4.11 and Annex D require that where voice evacuation is required, speech intelligibility be verified — typical benchmarks: STI ≥ 0.45 (or CIS ≥ 0.7) throughout the area of coverage; the STIPA measurement method must be identified at design [Source: NFPA 72-2022, §18.4.11 and Annex D; https://www.eaton.com/content/dam/eaton/products/safety-security-emergency-communications/lsmns/waves/documents/lsmns-intelligibility-white-paper.pdf].

**Operative implication for MWAA:** a quiet-by-design standard cannot lower ambient SPL, extend reverberation, or reduce loudspeaker density in a way that pushes the emergency-messaging envelope below the intelligibility floor. The right way to write the standard is to define the noise floor and reverberation *first*, then design the emergency voice system on top of that floor and re-verify STI at commissioning. A standard that inverts this order will be caught either by MWAA's own commissioning agent or by the local fire marshal at certificate of occupancy — one of the two most predictable ways a well-intentioned quiet program has died at U.S. airports in the last decade.

### 2.2 Accessibility law (also non-negotiable)

- **ADA Title II — 28 CFR Part 35, Subpart E** — MWAA, as an instrumentality of Virginia and D.C., is a Title II entity. §35.160 requires effective communication and "primary consideration" of the individual's requested aid; §35.161 covers TTY/telecommunications; §35.163 covers information and signage [Source: https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-28/chapter-I/part-35/subpart-E].
- **ADA Title III / Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act** — apply to tenants and to any federally assisted program at either airport.
- **Air Carrier Access Act (14 CFR Part 382)** — governs the airline's in-terminal communication to passengers. A quiet-by-design PA regime that suppresses airline gate announcements without providing an equally usable substitute exposes the *carrier* to Part 382 complaints, which they will push back to MWAA through the Use & Lease.
- **DOT rulemaking on accessible in-terminal information** — DOT's most recent PRM/ACAA rulemakings (14 CFR Part 382, revised 2024–2025) increased requirements around real-time information for passengers with disabilities. A quiet-by-design standard should be aligned to the DOT rule, not written as if it were the only accessibility framework in the room.

**Operative implication:** the standard must name at least three redundant information channels (audible, visual/digital, staffed) for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and irregular-operations message, and it must specify accessible formats by default, not by request. A visual-first design that requires passengers to opt in to a smartphone app is not "as effective" under §35.160.

### 2.3 FAA authority over MWAA design

- **Part 139** — airport operating certificate rules. Part 139 governs airfield operations and ARFF; interior terminal acoustics fall largely outside Part 139's four corners, but Part 139's incident-management and emergency-notification expectations flow into terminal communication design.
- **FAA AC 150/5360-13A, Airport Terminal Planning (2018)** — the guiding advisory circular for terminal design. Guidance, not rule; becomes rule via grant assurances when AIP or BIL money is in the funding stack [Source: https://www.faa.gov/documentLibrary/media/Advisory_Circular/AC-150-5360-13A-Airport-Terminal-Planning.pdf].
- **Grant assurances (49 U.S.C. §47107; standard FAA sponsor assurances)** — MWAA's acceptance of AIP and BIL Airport Terminal Program funds carries assurance obligations on economic nondiscrimination, competitive procurement, and design conformance. A design-manual change that governs federally funded projects is subject to those assurances; a design-manual change that governs only PFC- or bond-funded work has a shorter federal leash, but never a nonexistent one.
- **NEPA** — most interior renovations to existing terminals qualify for a Categorical Exclusion under FAA Order 1050.1F, provided they do not extend the building footprint, modify airfield geometry, or exceed the CATEX thresholds. Quiet-by-design is largely a CATEX-eligible interior scope, which is a schedule advantage worth naming; the schedule advantage disappears the moment the standard is used to justify additions to Concourse E, new Concourses C/D, or noise-mitigation work that touches the exterior.

### 2.4 TSA and CBP — sovereign tenants with veto over their own space

- **TSA checkpoint design standards** are governed by the TSA Checkpoint Design Guide (SSI/FOUO), plus the recent rollout of Credential Authentication Technology (CAT-2) and CT scanners. TSA's public-address, queue-management, and audio-command needs at the checkpoint are set by TSA and negotiated with the airport; MWAA cannot unilaterally quiet the checkpoint zone.
- **CBP Federal Inspection Services (FIS) technical design standards** at IAD govern the arrivals audio and signage environment. Any quiet-by-design change to the FIS envelope requires CBP concurrence and, in practice, CBP staffing capacity to compensate with staff-mediated communication.

**Operative implication:** TSA and CBP zones should be *excluded* from the mandatory acoustic thresholds and covered by a separate "coordination protocol" requirement in the standard. Airports that have not made this carve-out have ended up rewriting their standards after federal partners refused to sign off.

### 2.5 MWAA-specific governance and the airline contract

- **Metropolitan Washington Airports Act of 1986** — MWAA is federally chartered; the airports are federally leased. The Board has 17 members: Virginia's governor appoints seven (Virginia General Assembly confirms); the D.C. mayor appoints four (D.C. Council confirms); Maryland's governor appoints three; the President appoints three with U.S. Senate advice and consent [Source: airport-context.md §2].
- **MWAA v. CAAN (501 U.S. 252, 1991)** — Supreme Court invalidated the original Board of Review as unconstitutional; a reminder that any federal-control mechanism grafted onto MWAA can be tested and lost in court [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/501/252].
- **Perimeter and slot politics** — MWAA is on the record opposing perimeter relaxation and publishes a "Protecting the DCA Perimeter" position; the perimeter is defended by the Coalition to Protect America's Regional Airports (Virginia congressional delegation plus mid-continent airports). Section 502 of the 2024 FAA Reauthorization moved 10 exemptions; H.R. 3185 / S. 1933 had originally sought 56 [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/protecting-dca-perimeter; https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12504].
- **2025 Use & Lease** — signatory-airline residual model, $9.0 B CCP, 140% coverage target, expires December 31, 2039 [Source: airport-context.md §3]. MII thresholds for airline concurrence apply to capital projects; a design-manual change that alters program scope, cost, or the airline rate base runs through those thresholds. The MII language itself is not fully public and should be treated as an open question by any downstream recommendation.

