## 3. Case studies where regulation or politics determined the outcome

### 3.1 DCA slot exemptions, 2024 — politics determined the outcome, not economics
Every credible economist has argued that DCA's slot cap and 1,250-mile perimeter are inefficient. The 2024 Reauthorization debate opened with H.R. 3185 / S. 1933 proposing 56 new slots (28 daily round-trip services). It closed with Section 502 authorizing 10 exemptions (five daily round-trips) after MWAA, United, and the Virginia delegation moved as a bloc [Source: https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IN12504]. **Lesson for the Council:** the DCA operating envelope is fixed on our decision horizon. Do not write a quiet-by-design DCA case that assumes different traffic or a different slot regime.

### 3.2 PFC cap — four failed reauthorization attempts, 2017–2024
Airports for the last decade have made raising the PFC cap their top legislative priority. Every attempt has been defeated by Airlines for America. The cap has been $4.50 since April 1, 2001 [Source: https://www.faa.gov/airports/pfc]. **Lesson:** a quiet-by-design funding thesis must live within existing PFC scope amendment procedures (14 CFR Part 158), the airline rate base, or AIP/BIL Terminal Program discretionary awards. Do not premise the CIP business case on a PFC increase.

### 3.3 MWAA v. CAAN, 1991 — federal control at MWAA is legally fragile
The Board of Review, invented to give Congress a lever over MWAA capital decisions, was struck down. Congress has since chosen to constrain MWAA by statute (perimeter/slots, appropriations riders) rather than by grafted governance. **Lesson:** MWAA is unusually exposed to *statutory* federal action but unusually free of *federal-administrative* control. The White House July 2026 announcement is political, not legally binding — but on airport authority behavior at MWAA, political binding is usually enough.

### 3.4 The BWI silent-airport comparison as a cautionary tale
"Silent airport" branding has consistently outrun the operational governance to sustain it — every airport that has tried has quietly reintroduced boarding calls in the first irregular-ops event that produced a missed-connection wave. The relevant policy question for MWAA is not whether to go silent; it is whether to *codify a governance model* for when scripted PA is permitted, at what SPL, in what zones, with what STI verification, and with what redundant channels for accessibility.

